From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 11:43:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: apple: offer CPU PD only on M1 to M3

CPU PD sets bits in CYC_OVRD (s3_5_c15_c5_0). From the M4 generation on
iBoot locks that register and writing it is an undefined instruction.
The driver kept those SoCs off CPU PD with a deny list: an
"apple,t6041-cpuidle" or "apple,t8132-cpuidle" node, or a machine
compatible with "apple,t8140". Any other SoC that reached the probe got
CPU PD, so a later SoC whose device tree reused one of the node
compatibles, T6040 with its own node name or T8142 for instance, would
fault on its first deep idle.

Turn the test around: offer CPU PD only on the machines known to let
the OS write CYC_OVRD, M1 to M3 (T8103, T600x, T8112, T602x, T8122,
T603x), and run both entries through WFI everywhere else. These are
the SoCs aura also programs CYC_OVRD on, through its
apple_sysregs_unlocked feature; its M4-and-later feature set leaves the
register alone.

Every device tree in the tree gets the same states as before: M1 to M3
keep CPU PD, and T8132, T6040, T6041 and T8140 keep WFI for both. This
is the only CYC_OVRD user in the kernel.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
(cherry picked from commit ddb50b42408ee9ef0a1b957991fb5321b99ce686)
---
 drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c | 26 +++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c
index 896b92440e..e3751f23ae 100644
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c
@@ -122,21 +122,25 @@ static struct cpuidle_driver apple_idle_driver = {
 	.state_count = STATE_COUNT,
 };
 
+/* SoCs whose CYC_OVRD the OS can write: M1 to M3. */
+static const char *const apple_cyc_ovrd_machines[] = {
+	"apple,t8103", "apple,t6000", "apple,t6001", "apple,t6002",
+	"apple,t8112", "apple,t6020", "apple,t6021", "apple,t6022",
+	"apple,t8122", "apple,t6030", "apple,t6031", "apple,t6032",
+	"apple,t6034",
+	NULL
+};
+
 static int apple_cpuidle_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
-	struct device_node *np = pdev->dev.of_node;
-	bool wfi_only = of_device_is_compatible(np, "apple,t6041-cpuidle") ||
-			of_device_is_compatible(np, "apple,t8132-cpuidle");
-
 	/*
-	 * CPU PD writes CYC_OVRD, which is an undefined instruction on
-	 * T8140 and the M4 SoCs (T6041, T8132). Their WFI already lets
-	 * the hardware power-gate idle cores, so both entries must use
-	 * WFI. Keep index 1: the generic s2idle selector skips fallback
-	 * index 0, otherwise tick_freeze() and timekeeping suspension are
-	 * never reached.
+	 * CPU PD writes CYC_OVRD, which only M1 to M3 let the OS write. From
+	 * M4 on it is locked and the write is undefined, and WFI already
+	 * power-gates an idle core, so both entries use WFI. Keep index 1:
+	 * the generic s2idle selector skips fallback index 0, otherwise
+	 * tick_freeze() and timekeeping suspension are never reached.
 	 */
-	if (of_machine_is_compatible("apple,t8140") || wfi_only)
+	if (!of_machine_compatible_match(apple_cyc_ovrd_machines))
 		apple_idle_driver.states[STATE_PWRDOWN] = apple_idle_driver.states[STATE_WFI];
 
 	return cpuidle_register(&apple_idle_driver, NULL);
